      <p>You can install the NBDE Tang Operator either by using the web console or through the <code>oc</code> command from CLI.</p>
      <h2 id="installing-nbde-tang-server-operator-using-web-console_installing-nbde-tang-server-operator" class="group scroll-mt-20">Installing the NBDE Tang Server Operator using the web console<a href="#installing-nbde-tang-server-operator-using-web-console_installing-nbde-tang-server-operator" class="ml-2 text-muted-foreground opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 no-underline" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1">&para;</a></h2>
      <p>You can install the NBDE Tang Server Operator from the software catalog using the web console.</p>
      <p><strong>Prerequisites</strong></p>
      <ul><li>You must have <code>cluster-admin</code> privileges on an OpenShift Container Platform cluster.</li></ul>
      <p><strong>Procedure</strong></p>
      <ol>
      <li>In the OpenShift Container Platform web console, navigate to <strong>Ecosystem</strong> → <strong>Software Catalog</strong>.</li>
      <li>Search for the NBDE Tang Server Operator: <img src="/openshift-docs-markdown/images/nbde-tang-server-operator-01-operatorhub.png" alt="NBDE Tang Server Operator in the software catalog" loading="lazy" /></li>
      <li>Click <strong>Install</strong>.</li>
      <li>On the <strong>Operator Installation</strong> screen, keep the <strong>Update channel</strong>, <strong>Version</strong>, <strong>Installation mode</strong>, <strong>Installed Namespace</strong>, and <strong>Update approval</strong> fields on the default values.</li>
      <li>After you confirm the installation options by clicking <strong>Install</strong>, the console displays the installation confirmation. <img src="/openshift-docs-markdown/images/nbde-tang-server-operator-03-confirmation.png" alt="Confirmation of a NBDE Tang Server Operator installation" loading="lazy" /></li>
      </ol>
      <p><strong>Verification</strong></p>
      <ol>
      <li>Navigate to the <strong>Ecosystem</strong> → <strong>Installed Operators</strong> page.</li>
      <li>Check that the NBDE Tang Server Operator is installed and its status is <code>Succeeded</code>. <img src="/openshift-docs-markdown/images/nbde-tang-server-operator-05-succeeded.png" alt="NBDE Tang Server Operator status" loading="lazy" /></li>
      </ol>
      <h2 id="installing-nbde-tang-server-operator-using-cli_installing-nbde-tang-server-operator" class="group scroll-mt-20">Installing the NBDE Tang Server Operator using CLI<a href="#installing-nbde-tang-server-operator-using-cli_installing-nbde-tang-server-operator" class="ml-2 text-muted-foreground opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 no-underline" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1">&para;</a></h2>
      <p>You can install the NBDE Tang Server Operator from the software catalog using the CLI.</p>
      <p><strong>Prerequisites</strong></p>
      <ul>
      <li>You must have <code>cluster-admin</code> privileges on an OpenShift Container Platform cluster.</li>
      <li>You have installed the OpenShift CLI (<code>oc</code>).</li>
      </ul>
      <p><strong>Procedure</strong></p>
      <ol>
      <li>
      Use the following command to list available Operators in the software catalog, and limit the output to Tang-related results:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc get packagemanifests -n openshift-marketplace | grep tang"} lang="terminal" />
      <CodeBlock code={"tang-operator           Red Hat"} lang="terminal" title="Example output" />
      <p>In this case, the corresponding packagemanifest name is <code>tang-operator</code>.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
      Create a <code>Subscription</code> object YAML file to subscribe a namespace to the NBDE Tang Server Operator, for example, <code>tang-operator.yaml</code>:
      <CodeBlock code={"apiVersion: operators.coreos.com/v1alpha1\nkind: Subscription\nmetadata:\n  name: tang-operator\n  namespace: openshift-operators\nspec:\n  channel: stable (1)\n  installPlanApproval: Automatic\n  name: tang-operator (2)\n  source: redhat-operators (3)\n  sourceNamespace: openshift-marketplace <4> "} lang="yaml" title="Example subscription YAML for tang-operator" />
      <ol>
      <li>Specify the channel name from where you want to subscribe the Operator.</li>
      <li>Specify the name of the Operator to subscribe to.</li>
      <li>Specify the name of the CatalogSource that provides the Operator.</li>
      <li>The namespace of the CatalogSource. Use <code>openshift-marketplace</code> for the default software catalog sources.</li>
      </ol>
      </li>
      <li>
      Apply the <code>Subscription</code> to the cluster:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc apply -f tang-operator.yaml"} lang="terminal" />
      </li>
      </ol>
      <p><strong>Verification</strong></p>
      <ul>
      <li>
      Check that the NBDE Tang Server Operator controller runs in the <code>openshift-operators</code> namespace:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc -n openshift-operators get pods"} lang="terminal" />
      <CodeBlock code={"NAME                                                READY   STATUS    RESTARTS   AGE\ntang-operator-controller-manager-694b754bd6-4zk7x   2/2     Running   0          12s"} lang="terminal" title="Example output" />
      </li>
      </ul>
